= April 2026 — Channels, not a Left-appointed successor

<span class="party-ko"><b>[+ ko_leader +]</b></span> holds KO's national leadership. The question is not a
vacancy Lewica may fill; it is a division of access beneath the leader.[? if ko_consolidated:
 The consolidated KO party assigns national offices among networks that once
belonged to separate component organisations.?][? if not ko_consolidated: PO,
KO and the surviving component organisations bargain over common offices while
retaining their legal identities.?]

<span class="party-ko"><b>Barbara Nowacka</b></span>, KO's education-policy progressive, offers cooperation on
rights and public services. <span class="party-ko"><b>Radosław Sikorski</b></span>, its Atlanticist foreign-policy
voice, offers Washington and European security channels. <span class="party-ko"><b>Marcin Kierwiński</b></span>,
the party organiser, offers lists, county structures and quiet coalition
management. **[+ ko_leader +]** offers one accountable principal and no
insurance against what happens after them. [? if ko_leader != "Donald Tusk":
Whoever holds the chair now holds it because KO's 2025 convention decided it
could not keep the leader it had, which is exactly why the currents below are
still competing.?]

Lewica must choose which relationship it wants to deepen. The choice shifts
trust and influence inside KO; it does not appoint its leader or turn a contact
into a coalition agreement.

- @ko_nowacka
- @ko_sikorski
- @ko_kierwinski
- @ko_tusk

