= June 2026 — A ruling across the institutional divide

The Constitutional Tribunal led by <span class=""><b>Bogdan Święczkowski</b></span> says that **[+
president_name +]** holds greater personal authority over Supreme Court
appointments and leadership. The decision does not settle the dispute. It
deepens it: one side calls K 2/26 a constitutional judgment, the other calls
the panel itself unlawfully composed.

[? if war_top_camp = "none" and government_party != "pis": Justice Minister
**[+ justice_minister +]** says the applicant lacked standing and the ruling
has no legal effect.?][? if war_top_camp = "none" and government_party = "pis":
The PiS cabinet accepts the expansion of presidential power.?][? if
war_top_camp != "none": The
cabinet does not fight the ruling, because the man it empowers belongs to the
governing camp. Justice Minister **[+ justice_minister +]** welcomes it in one
sentence and says nothing else. Inside the camp, nobody mistakes what has
happened: a president who already believes he is more popular than his own
prime minister has been handed a power the Chancellery cannot take back.?]
[? if justice_minister_party = "Lewica": Lewica controls the ministry
that must now choose between appearing, ignoring the decision, legislating
around it or bargaining with the Palace.?][? if justice_minister_party !=
"Lewica": Lewica can press its case in Parliament and court filings, but **[+
justice_minister +]**, not the party, commands the ministry.?]

[? if war_top_camp != "none": Commentators reach for the precedent within the
hour. The first war at the top was 1990, Wałęsa against Mazowiecki. The one
this resembles is 2003: **[+ war_top_president +]** and **[+ war_top_pm +]**,
one camp, one falling government, and a presidency deciding whether to save it
or replace it.?]

- @competence_appear
- @competence_ignore
- @competence_legislate
- @competence_bargain
- @competence_opposition_brief
- @competence_opposition_record

@competence_appear
title: Challenge the panel while appearing and publishing every argument
subtitle: Let the Lewica Justice Ministry contest jurisdiction without leaving the President's case unanswered.
view-if: left_in_government = 1 and justice_minister_party = "Lewica"
on-arrival: government_procedural_restraint += 5; tribunal_domestic_recognition += 1; legal_dualism += 1; court_user_trust += 2; president_relation -= 2; news_headline = "The government contests jurisdiction without pretending the case does not exist"

[+ justice_minister +]'s ministry files its objection, appears under protest and publishes the
submission before the hearing. The act of appearing gives the panel no clean
claim that government silence conceded its authority; it also concedes enough
procedure for critics to say the ministry has recognised a court it calls
invalid. Court users at least receive one complete public argument.

- @poland_event_queue: Appear under protest

@competence_ignore
title: Treat the decision as a non-judgment and continue
subtitle: Refuse every legal consequence of K 2/26 and accept two branches acting under incompatible constitutions.
view-if: left_in_government = 1 and justice_minister_party = "Lewica"
on-arrival: tribunal_domestic_recognition -= 7; legal_dualism += 8; government_procedural_restraint -= 5; judicial_repair_mandate += 3; president_relation -= 6; news_headline = "Two branches act under incompatible versions of the constitution"

The ministry issues appointments and instructions as though K 2/26 had never
been delivered. The Palace answers as though those acts never occurred. Lewica
pleases lawyers who reject Święczkowski's Tribunal, but judges, clerks and
litigants now have to guess which version of state authority will reach their
case first.

- @poland_event_queue: Sign the appointments anyway

@competence_legislate
title: Return with a general appointments and reasons statute
subtitle: Replace the fight over one office with a rule that binds every future appointment, even at the price of delay.
view-if: left_in_government = 1 and justice_minister_party = "Lewica"
on-arrival: government_procedural_restraint += 5; constitutional_restraint += 4; judicial_vacancies += 2; court_backlog += 2; president_relation -= 2; news_headline = "A general law replaces the temptation to win one personnel case"

The Justice Ministry submits a general statute requiring published reasons,
fixed deadlines and the same appointment procedure regardless of who holds the
Palace. Vacancies remain open while Parliament works. That delay angers court
users, yet it denies Lewica the convenient fiction that a personnel victory is
the same thing as a constitutional repair.

- @poland_event_queue: Submit the general statute

@competence_bargain
title: Negotiate a temporary appointments protocol with the Palace
subtitle: Use a viable presidential relationship to clear vacant benches without asking either side to recognise the other's whole legal theory.
choose-if: president_relation >= 35
unavailable-subtitle: The President prefers the ruling to a compromise.
view-if: left_in_government = 1 and justice_minister_party = "Lewica"
on-arrival: president_relation += 7; president_judiciary_cooperation += 6; judicial_vacancies -= 3; legal_dualism -= 3; government_procedural_restraint += 2; progressives_dissent += 2; news_headline = "A temporary appointments protocol clears some benches"

The ministry and Palace sign a temporary sequence for nominations, written
reasons and disputed files. Neither side recognises the other's account of K
2/26, but several empty benches can be filled without another institutional
collision. Lewica's constitutionalists call it breathing space; its purists
call it a truce with an unlawful order.

- @poland_event_queue: Sign the temporary protocol

@competence_opposition_brief
title: File a Lewica brief and demand a general appointments law
subtitle: Without the Justice Ministry, Lewica can litigate and legislate but cannot command the government's response. Cost: 1 resource.
view-if: left_in_government = 0 or justice_minister_party != "Lewica"
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The constitutional litigation team cannot be financed.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; constitutional_restraint += 4; judicial_repair_mandate += 2; court_user_trust += 2; media_capacity += 2; news_headline = "Lewica publishes an institutional alternative from opposition"

Lewica's lawyers file a brief against the expanded power and attach a draft
appointments statute that would bind both ministry and President. [? if
left_in_government: Coalition partners must now answer an argument from inside
their own majority, but the serving Justice Minister remains free to reject
it.?][? if not left_in_government: The government can ignore an opposition
brief; it cannot claim that no workable alternative was offered.?]

- @poland_event_queue: File the brief

@competence_opposition_record
title: Put the constitutional objection on the Sejm record
subtitle: Record a parliamentary position without pretending that Lewica controls the ministry or the President.
view-if: left_in_government = 0 or justice_minister_party != "Lewica"
on-arrival: constitutional_restraint += 1; judicial_repair_mandate += 1; news_headline = "Lewica records its objection to the Tribunal ruling in the Sejm"

The club reads its objection into the Sejm record and names the appointment
rule it would support. No vacancy is filled and no state institution changes
course. What remains is a dated position that future ministers, judges and
Lewica itself can be made to answer for.

